      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Major Utah Cost Drivers"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Tear-Off vs. Layering: Completely removing your old shingles and throwing them away typically adds $1 to $3 per square foot to the bill. However, layering new shingles over old ones voids most manufacturer warranties and traps heat.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Roof Pitch: Steep roofs require extra safety harnesses, specialized scaffolding, and more labor hours, pushing your overall pricing higher.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Weatherproofing Add-ons: Utah's heavy winter snow means most local code regulations require extra ice and water shield protective barriers along your roof's eaves and valleys to stop ice damming."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          13,
          14,
          15
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help narrow down this range, could you tell me the approximate square footage of your home, its general location or county in Utah, and what roofing material you are leaning toward?"
